nodecode-woof is a rewrite of recastsystems's recastsystems/hermes-woof:

  https://github.com/recastsystems/hermes-woof
  commit 2c82b34eb386a490d25439757556f6bab84048af
  Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) 2026 recastsystems (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: all twelve of the original's tool verbs. The doctor that
names the command; the catalog that asks WOOF what it offers; the documentation
search, reimplemented as the original's own BM25 over the pack the original
ships (docs/index.json and docs/MANIFEST.json here, carried unchanged apart
from the manifest's unused keys, and built from WOOF 1.0.0's documentation at
source commit ee21e5d3896bd6e10e3f461d9563921fc149d49f, which is also the
evaluator commit a one-off score's registration names); the plan that asks WOOF to resolve and
estimate a run; the launch that starts it detached; the status and the stop
that read and signal the run's own folder and pid; the show and the compare
that answer maps as MEDIA: lines; the campaign that sizes a set of arms along
one axis and runs them one at a time on the one card; the score that puts a
finished run against MRMS, Stage-IV and ASOS observations under a one-off
registration; and the report that gathers what a run or a campaign left behind.
With them: the run folder layout (plan.json, woof.log, events.jsonl, the
rendered maps, score.json), the campaign folder layout, the section that
points the cell at the WOOF command and the runs folder, and the manual the
model reads. The run machinery is Common Lisp calling the `woof` command the
machine already has; the original's Python is not bundled and none of it runs
here.

The one part that speaks to WOOF's Python: `woof_score`. The original scores by
running the platform wheel's own modules -- `tools.obs_fetch_mrms`,
`tools.obs_fetch_stage4`, `tools.obs_fetch_asos` and `tools.obs_battery_score`
-- in the WOOF environment, under a registration built by
`woof.verify.obs.registration`. The rewrite runs the same modules, with the
same arguments and the same fallbacks, through the interpreter that runs the
`woof` command, and asks that same registration library for the document in one
short call. It is WOOF's own code being driven, not the plugin's.

What was left out, and why: the watcher, which polled the run folders in a
background thread and pushed each event into the chat as a message. Its job is
done here by polling `woof_status`, so nothing is injected into a turn.
`hermes woof install`, which pip-installs WOOF into a private virtual
environment, and the ssh and WSL executors, which drive a GPU host somewhere
else, are out too: this cell runs the command the machine it is on already has.
The optional local model server the repository ships (llama.cpp and a GGUF,
downloaded by its own shell scripts) is not part of the rewrite at all, and
neither is the plugin's own campaign index, which the campaign folder replaces.
